---
import DraftPage from '../components/draft/DraftPage.astro';
import PostCard from '../components/PostCard.astro';
import ProcessStage from '../components/ProcessStage.astro';
import { posts } from '../data/posts';

type Tone = 'grey' | 'blue' | 'purple' | 'green';

// The six-box board: the top row is the problem, the bottom row is the solution.
const boardRows: { label: string; boxes: { number: number; name: string; tone: Tone }[] }[] = [
	{
		label: 'The problem',
		boxes: [
			{ number: 1, name: 'Problem and success', tone: 'grey' },
			{ number: 2, name: 'Who and what we know', tone: 'blue' },
			{ number: 3, name: 'Journey and pain', tone: 'blue' },
		],
	},
	{
		label: 'The solution',
		boxes: [
			{ number: 4, name: 'Riskiest assumption and bet', tone: 'purple' },
			{ number: 5, name: 'Ideas and screens', tone: 'purple' },
			{ number: 6, name: 'Test and measure', tone: 'green' },
		],
	},
];

const situations = [
	{
		name: 'New or fuzzy.',
		text: "The goal isn't clear yet. Before talking to customers, I partner with the product manager to talk to stakeholders and agree what success means. The problem side of the board is where most of the time goes.",
	},
	{
		name: 'Known problem.',
		text: 'The data shows where people struggle, but not why. I start with what we already know, then talk to a handful of customers to find the why.',
	},
	{
		name: 'Ongoing work.',
		text: "The work is already moving, often with research done and a solution planned. I catch up on what's been decided, build on the existing research rather than redo it, and treat the planned solution as a bet to test.",
	},
	{
		name: 'Small change.',
		text: 'Clear and low risk. A quick look at the data, a few options, a light test, and one number to check afterwards.',
	},
];

const guesses = [
	{ name: 'Desirable', question: 'will people want it?' },
	{ name: 'Usable', question: 'can they use it?' },
	{ name: 'Buildable', question: 'can we make it?' },
	{ name: 'Viable', question: 'does it work for the business?' },
];

---

<DraftPage
	title="My design process | Andrew Smith"
	description="How I take a vague request to a measured result, in six stages, and how the process changes when work is fuzzy, already moving, or small."
	ogType="article"
>
	<article class="process-page">
		<header class="draft-article-hero">
			<p class="eyebrow">Process</p>
			<h1 class="display-title">How I work</h1>
		</header>

		<div class="draft-article">
			<p>
				Most work doesn't arrive as a clear problem. It arrives as a request: "we need fewer calls", "can we add a
				chatbot", "make the button bigger". My job is to work out what is really going on, find the riskiest guess
				behind the idea, test it cheaply, and then measure whether the change made a difference.
			</p>
			<p>
				I use the same six stages on every piece of work. They are based on the Double Diamond (first make sure we're
				solving the right problem, then make sure we're building the right solution), and I run them in short loops
				alongside the product team: think, make, check, then go round again.
			</p>
			<p>
				What changes from project to project is how deep I go in each stage. A small label change might take an
				afternoon. A fuzzy new problem might need weeks of discovery. The stages stay the same.
			</p>
		</div>

		<figure class="process-board">
			{
				boardRows.map((row) => (
					<div class="process-board-row">
						<p class="process-board-label">{row.label}</p>
						<ol start={row.boxes[0].number}>
							{row.boxes.map((box) => (
								<li data-tone={box.tone}>
									<span class="process-board-number" aria-hidden="true">
										{box.number}
									</span>
									{box.name}
								</li>
							))}
						</ol>
					</div>
				))
			}
			<figcaption>
				The board I set up at the start of every piece of work, in Miro or on a whiteboard. The top row is the problem.
				The bottom row is the solution. You can't skip to the bottom row without filling in the top.
			</figcaption>
		</figure>

		<ProcessStage
			number={1}
			name="Kickoff"
			question="What are we trying to change, and for whom?"
			activities={[
				'Kickoff meeting',
				'Success measure and baseline',
				'Limits and constraints',
				'Checking a solution already in mind',
				'Deciding the situation',
			]}
		>
			<p class="process-stage-lead">Agree the problem and how we'll know it worked.</p>
			<p>
				Every piece of work starts with a kickoff with the product manager, the engineer, and usually the content
				designer and business analyst. I ask six questions:
			</p>
			<ol class="process-question-card">
				<li>What are we trying to change, and for whom?</li>
				<li>How will we know it worked?</li>
				<li>What is the number today?</li>
				<li>What must not get worse?</li>
				<li>What can't change?</li>
				<li>Is a solution already in mind, and which problem would it solve?</li>
			</ol>
			<p>
				The number today is the one most teams skip. Without a baseline, nobody can ever show the work made a
				difference. The "must not get worse" list protects us from winning one number by breaking another, like
				selling more add-ons but getting more complaints.
			</p>
			<p>
				If someone arrives with a solution, I don't argue with it. I park it, and we come back to it once we
				understand the problem. Often it turns out to be a good idea. Sometimes it solves the wrong thing.
			</p>
			<p>
				The gaps in those answers tell me what kind of work this is, and that decides how much of the rest of the
				process we need.
			</p>
		</ProcessStage>

		<section class="process-wide" aria-labelledby="kinds-heading">
			<div class="draft-article">
				<h2 id="kinds-heading">Four kinds of work, one process</h2>
				<p>At the end of kickoff I decide which of four situations we're in.</p>
				<ul class="process-cards process-cards-2">
					{
						situations.map((situation) => (
							<li class="process-card-item">
								<strong>{situation.name}</strong> {situation.text}
							</li>
						))
					}
				</ul>
				<p>Some work also takes a detour:</p>
				<ul>
					<li>
						<strong>When a solution is brought in,</strong> I run it through the bet stage as a hypothesis instead of
						building it on trust.
					</li>
					<li>
						<strong>For AI or automation,</strong> I test with a person behind the scenes before anything is built,
						and design for the moments it gets things wrong.
					</li>
					<li>
						<strong>When the deadline is urgent,</strong> I never skip the problem or the baseline. Everything else
						shrinks.
					</li>
				</ul>
			</div>
		</section>

		<ProcessStage
			number={2}
			name="Understand"
			question="Who is struggling, where, and why?"
			activities={[
				'Evidence review',
				'Stakeholder interviews',
				'Customer interviews',
				'Call recordings and shadowing',
				'Current flow mapping',
				'Journey mapping',
			]}
		>
			<p>
				The golden rule is to start with what we already know. The answer is often already in an analytics
				dashboard, the contact centre's call reasons, customer feedback, past research or the journey management
				platform. I split what I find into two lists: what we know (with the source next to each finding) and what
				we don't know yet. Every gap gets a method and a person: interviews, watching call recordings, shadowing
				frontline staff, or walking through the journey myself.
			</p>
			<p>
				An interview gives me a lead, and the data confirms it. When two sources disagree, I find out why and use a
				third source to settle it.
			</p>
			<p>
				Then I lay out the journey as customers actually experience it, not how the system thinks it works. For
				each step I note what people are doing and feeling, and, alongside the engineer and business analyst, what
				system or business rule sits behind it. That system lane is where the real cause often hides. In one
				example, customers kept calling to check on their refund. The status existed in the system, and the
				contact centre could see it. The customer couldn't.
			</p>
			<p>
				I circle the cause, not the symptom. The calls were the symptom. The silence during the wait was the
				cause.
			</p>
			<p>This stage ends when I can finish one sentence with evidence:</p>
			<p class="process-pullquote">These people struggle at this step because of this reason.</p>
			<p>
				The journey on my board is a snapshot of the steps that matter for this problem. The full journey lives in
				the organisation's journey management platform, shared with service design and the customer experience
				team. What I learn goes back into it, so the next team starts from a better place.
			</p>
		</ProcessStage>

		<ProcessStage
			number={3}
			name="Explore"
			question="What are we betting on?"
			activities={[
				'How might we',
				'Assumption mapping',
				'Writing the hypothesis',
				'Crazy 8s',
				'Dot voting and decision log',
				'Design sprints for big bets',
			]}
		>
			<p class="process-stage-lead">Make the bet visible.</p>
			<p>
				Before anyone draws a screen, I turn the problem sentence into an open question that starts with "How might
				we", then sketch a few rough directions and pick one.
			</p>
			<p>
				Then I run a short assumption-mapping workshop with the product trio. Everyone finishes the sentence "For
				this to work, it has to be true that..." on sticky notes, grouped into four kinds of guess:
			</p>
			<ul class="process-cards process-cards-4">
				{
					guesses.map((guess) => (
						<li class="process-card-item">
							<strong>{guess.name}:</strong> {guess.question}
						</li>
					))
				}
			</ul>
			<p>
				We place each note on a simple grid: how important it is, against how much evidence we already have.
				Anything important with little evidence goes in the top-left corner, and we test it first, with a name next
				to each test. The riskiest guess is often not about the customer at all. It's whether the system can do
				what the idea needs, so the engineer checks it before any design work.
			</p>
			<figure class="assumption-map">
				<p class="draft-sr-only">
					A grid of importance against evidence. Important guesses with little evidence sit in the top-left
					quarter, marked test first.
				</p>
				<div class="assumption-map-chart" aria-hidden="true">
					<span class="assumption-map-y">Important</span>
					<div class="assumption-map-grid">
						<span class="assumption-map-hot">Test first</span>
						<span></span>
						<span></span>
						<span></span>
					</div>
					<span class="assumption-map-x">
						<span>Little evidence</span>
						<span>Strong evidence</span>
					</span>
				</div>
			</figure>
			<p>Then I write the bet as a hypothesis the whole team agrees with:</p>
			<div class="process-callout">
				<p class="process-callout-label">The hypothesis</p>
				<p>
					We believe <strong>[the change]</strong> for <strong>[these people]</strong> will{' '}
					<strong>[this outcome]</strong>. We'll know when <strong>[this number moves]</strong>. We're wrong if{' '}
					<strong>[this happens]</strong>.
				</p>
			</div>
			<p>
				The "we're wrong if" line is what makes it scientific. We decide what failure looks like before we start,
				so nobody can spin the result afterwards.
			</p>
			<p>
				Only then do we generate ideas. I use quick, structured methods like Crazy 8s (eight sketches in eight
				minutes) so everyone's ideas count equally, and dot voting to choose. For big, risky decisions, I run a full
				design sprint. Every decision goes in a decision log, with the reason, so it doesn't get argued twice.
			</p>
			<p>
				This is also where the design system earns its keep. For a quick change, most of the answer is already in
				existing components and patterns, so I sketch straight from the system. For something new, I start from
				the system and only design new components where the problem needs them.
			</p>
		</ProcessStage>

		<ProcessStage
			number={4}
			name="Test"
			question="Does it work for people?"
			activities={[
				'Choosing the method and fidelity',
				'Usability testing',
				'Tree testing',
				'Testing AI with a person behind the scenes',
				'Accessibility check',
			]}
		>
			<p class="process-stage-lead">Check the bet cheaply, before it's built.</p>
			<p>
				I make the prototype only as real as the question needs. Rough sketches answer "is this the right idea?". A
				clickable prototype answers "can people find their way?". Real content and real prices answer "do they
				trust it?". A tree test answers "where does this belong in the menu?". For AI, a person quietly playing the
				system answers "how should it behave?".
			</p>
			<p>
				I plan, recruit and run the sessions, usually with five or six people from the group we're designing for,
				including people who use assistive technology. I watch what people do, not what they say they like. The
				product manager joins the sessions and the engineers observe when they can. Then we fix what failed and
				retest with a smaller group. Accessibility gets checked before anything goes near a sprint.
			</p>
		</ProcessStage>

		<ProcessStage
			number={5}
			name="Deliver"
			question="Are we building it well?"
			activities={[
				'Designing every state',
				'Design system specs and tokens',
				'Refinement walkthrough',
				'Design QA',
				'Release checkpoint',
			]}
		>
			<p class="process-stage-lead">Build it the way it was tested.</p>
			<p>
				I stay in the squad through the build, working a sprint or two ahead. Before refinement, I design every
				state: loading, error, empty and success, plus the awkward edge cases the engineers raise. The specs
				reference the design system's components and design tokens, so engineers build from the same source of
				truth. New components we've tested go back into the design system with their documentation and
				accessibility notes.
			</p>
			<p>
				When something has to give mid-sprint, I protect the customer outcome over pixels, and log the trade-off.
				Once it's in a test environment, I check the build against the design. Before release, I confirm two
				things: the baseline has been captured, and tracking works. Without them, we can't measure anything.
			</p>
		</ProcessStage>

		<ProcessStage
			number={6}
			name="Measure and learn"
			question="Did it work?"
			activities={['Results against the baseline', 'Frontline check-in', 'Benchmarking', 'Playback', 'Looping back']}
		>
			<p>
				A few weeks after release, we compare the main number against the baseline from kickoff and check that the
				guardrails held. I also ask the frontline teams what they're hearing, because they often notice a change
				before the dashboards do. Over time, regular benchmarking shows whether the experience is getting better or
				worse.
			</p>
			<p>
				Then we play it back: what we believed, what we did, what happened, and what's next. That decides where we
				loop back to. If we had the wrong problem, back to understanding it. If we had the wrong idea, back to
				exploring. If it worked, on to the next problem. The results go into the journey management platform, so
				the whole organisation can see what changed.
			</p>
		</ProcessStage>

		<section class="process-wide" aria-labelledby="bigger-picture-heading">
			<div class="draft-article">
				<h2 id="bigger-picture-heading">The bigger picture</h2>
				<p>
					A single project is one loop. The work that lasts is what connects the loops. Each project adds to a
					shared picture of the experience in the journey management platform, alongside service design, customer
					experience research, analytics and benchmarks. Each new pattern strengthens the design system. Over time,
					that means teams start from what's already known instead of from scratch, and quick work can be done well
					without a designer in every decision.
				</p>
				<p>
					That's the part of the job I find most interesting: not just solving this problem, but leaving the
					organisation better at solving the next one.
				</p>
			</div>
		</section>
	</article>

	<section class="draft-related-posts" aria-labelledby="related-heading">
		<h2 id="related-heading">Read more about how I work</h2>
		<ul class="post-list">
			{
				posts.map((post) => (
					<li>
						<PostCard post={post} />
					</li>
				))
			}
		</ul>
	</section>
</DraftPage>
